nction of x1 x2 and x3  similarly for u2 and similarly for u3  so what we can say  is that q is actually  a function of x  and that is given by  this  okay and we all understand that uh  with reference to our previous diagrams  this u vector this displacement vector  is the  uh the is actually the displacement  vector of the position of the  point p now all this is good  but in order to actually quantify the  displacement  what will required is uh is to compare  the positions  of two neighboring points p and q so all  this while we have been  uh have been dealing with uh  perhaps it will be clearer uh if i i  just quickly draw the diagram that i had  drawn  previously so if we had uh  if we can consider this  three-dimensional body  what we had done earlier was to consider  this point p  which had the position vector x and this  was in the undeformed state  and as it deforms and goes to the  current state  material point p  it goes and becomes p dash and  its position vector is given by the  small x and  we have what we have basically written  here  is that the displacement vector of the q  this is of this point p  all right now as i was  saying in order to actually quantify the  deformation in a proper fashion  what we need to do is consider another  point which is just beside  t may be depicted by the point q and  then to consider  the relative uh displacement between the  t and the q so that is what we are going  to do next  okay so  quantification of deformation  we again consider our three-dimensional  body  with the point p here but  we also consider another point q  which is a neighbor of the point p and  depict  its position vector like this  x plus d x vector  now this is in the undeformed state as  always  and this goes  and becomes in the deformed state  t dash depicted as before by  our small x and the q becomes  q dash represented by small  x plus e small  x this is our current state  to properly go ahead with the  quantification of deformation  what we are going to do is we are going  to consider  the displacement vector not of the  position of the point p  rather of the point q so that will be  given by  u vector not of  not a function of x because we are not  going to consider the displacement  vector of the point p we are going to  consider the displacement vector  of the point q so that is given by  x plus dx  and that by definition should be equal  to  the final position vector  given by x plus dx  minus the initial position vector  capital x plus dx  however we know from our previous  discussions  that  the displacement vector for the point p  as depicted by u being a function of x  that is nothing but small x minus  capital x now you note that in this  previous equation  this small x minus capital x is very  much present  because x is here and capital x is here  so we may write from here  that q vector  or the point q  is this displacement vector  for the point p plus  the rest of it just as before  okay so  from there we can quickly write down  this statement that d small  x vector is equal to d capital x vector  plus  the displacement vector for the point q  minus the displacement vector for the  point  p now as far as  the quantification of deformation is  concerned  this is the statement that we wanted to  come to  however this is not in a very convenient  form what you would like to do  is to carry out some mathematical  manipulations  over this difference between the two  displacement vectors  and simplify this expression to a more  convenient  and more physically interpretable form  so  that will be done in the next part of  the lecture thank you

